    "lawCode": "Amd §14, Transp L (as proposed in S.199-B & A.4010-B)",
    "lawSection": "Transportation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7791\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the transportation law, in relation to requiring the\ndepartment of transportation to annually prepare and submit to the\nlegislature certain highway pavement and bridge condition reports\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to make various technical amendments to a\nlaw adopted in 2021 requiring the Department of Transportation (DOT) to\nsubmit annual reports on capital project lettings, capital program\naccomplishments, highway bridge and pavement condition reports, and\ntransit capital disbursements.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: The bill modifies the previous chapter to authorize the due\ndate for the capital project letting reports to be June first, which is\nmore consistent with DOT's project planning schedule. This enables DOT\nto provide reporting after the State Budget is adopted, when it has a\nclearer understanding of its appropriations. The bill also clarifies the\nranges required for cost estimates, consistent with the federal planning\n(MPO) process.\n\nThe bill also clarifies that DOT's report on disbursements toward trans-\nit capital shall exclude the Metropolitan Transportation Authority\n(MTA), which has its own similar capital program reporting requirements,\nand to clarify that DOT's reporting requirement includes disbursements\nfor which it is responsible. Transit operators also apply for separate\ngrants and loans from the Federal government that may not pass through\nDOT.\n\nSection 2 contains the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation seeks to help inform the Legislature and public as to\nwhere funds are needed and are being appropriated to help repair and\nmaintain New York's roads and bridges. A similar concept was included as\npart of the DOT 2015-2019 five-year capital plan. This legislation seeks\nto codify this report into the Transportation Law. Having this informa-\ntion readily available will help pinpoint and address the roads and\nbridges that need repair, while ensuring that the public can feel\ncertain that the highways and bridges they use are being addressed to\nthe best capabilities of New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021: Underlying legislation (S.199-B/A.4010-B) passed both houses unan-\nimously and was signed by the Executive.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNo negative impacts anticipated for state or local governments.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date as the underlying chapter,\nwhich was immediate.\n",
